.\" Render with: groff -man -Tutf8 smithproxy-mitm.7
.TH SMITHPROXY-MITM 7 "OCT 2026" "Smithproxy" "Miscellaneous Information Manual"
.SH NAME
smithproxy-mitm \- Smithproxy interception and inspection model
.SH DESCRIPTION
Smithproxy is a policy-driven man-in-the-middle proxy. For an accepted
connection it maintains two independent transport legs: one facing the client
and one facing the upstream server. Data is inspected and relayed between them
according to the matching policy and its profiles.
.PP
The short model is:
.nf
    client <== left leg ==> Smithproxy <== right leg ==> server
.fi
.PP
For plaintext traffic Smithproxy relays application bytes directly. For TLS,
SSH or QUIC MITM it terminates encryption on both legs, processes plaintext in
the middle and establishes separate cryptographic sessions toward each peer.
The client therefore authenticates Smithproxy, not the original server key.
.SH CONNECTION MODEL
The two legs have separate sockets, handshakes, buffers, error states and
cryptographic parameters. A single logical session joins them for policy,
accounting, capture and diagnostics.
.PP
Smithproxy can accept traffic through:
.TP
.B TPROXY
Routed Linux traffic intercepted while retaining the original destination.
Policies may preserve the original client source address upstream when routing
and kernel configuration allow it.
.TP
.B REDIRECT
Traffic originated on the Smithproxy host. TCP destinations can be recovered;
local UDP interception is primarily useful for DNS because REDIRECT does not
retain a general original UDP destination.
.TP
.B SOCKS4/SOCKS5
Explicit proxy connections. Client-supplied addresses or hostnames are passed
through the normal policy and inspection path.
.TP
.B HTTP CONNECT
Explicit HTTP tunnel requests. After a successful upstream connection, the
tunnel follows the same TLS detection, policy and relay path as other TCP
connections.
.SH POLICY SELECTION
Traffic is matched against the ordered
.B policy
list. Match fields refer to named protocol, address and port objects. The first
matching rule supplies the action, NAT behavior, routing entry and inspection
profiles.
.PP
Profiles separate the decision to accept a connection from the way it is
inspected. Relevant assignments include:
.TP
.B tls_profile
TLS interception, certificate validation and TLS behavior.
.TP
.B ssh_profile
SSHv2 MITM behavior on builds with libssh support.
.TP
.B detection_profile
Protocol engines and metadata collection.
.TP
.B content_profile
Capture and content processing.
.TP
.B alg_dns_profile
DNS transaction processing.
.PP
A connection accepted without an inspection profile can still be forwarded as
opaque TCP or UDP. Denied traffic is not connected upstream.
.SH TLS MITM
When TLS inspection is selected, Smithproxy behaves as a TLS server toward the
client and as a TLS client toward the upstream server:
.nf
    TLS client == TLS A ==> Smithproxy == TLS B ==> TLS server
                         plaintext
.fi
.PP
TLS A and TLS B have independent keys and session state. Smithproxy reads the
ClientHello, including SNI where available, connects upstream, evaluates the
server certificate and presents a replacement certificate to the client. The
replacement is signed by the configured Smithproxy CA or selected from custom
certificate material.
.PP
Clients performing normal certificate validation must trust the Smithproxy CA.
Certificate pinning, private trust stores and applications which do not use the
system trust store can reject the replacement certificate even when the CA is
installed system-wide.
.SS Certificate selection
The certificate shown downstream can be generated from upstream identity data
and cached for reuse. Configuration can also select certificates by SNI or
destination address. Protect the CA private key: possession of that key permits
impersonation to every client which trusts the CA.
.SS Upstream validation
The right leg validates the actual upstream certificate according to its TLS
profile and configured CA bundle. Profiles control handling of untrusted
issuers, invalid or self-signed certificates, OCSP, stapling, CRLs and optional
Certificate Transparency data.
.PP
An upstream validation failure can close the connection or produce a configured
warning response on suitable HTTP traffic. Permissive validation options reduce
security and should be limited to policies which require them.
.SS Detection and bypass
Known TLS listener ports can be directed to the TLS acceptor. TLS autodetection
can also recognize ClientHello traffic on unusual ports. A policy with a TLS
profile whose inspection is disabled forwards TLS without terminating it.
.PP
SNI-based bypass is a policy decision, not a cryptographic guarantee. DNS cache
and domain-tree assistance can associate an address with configured names; stale
or ambiguous DNS data must be considered when designing bypass rules.
.SH STARTTLS
STARTTLS begins as plaintext and upgrades an existing application connection to
TLS after a protocol-specific request and response. Smithproxy uses configured
STARTTLS signatures to recognize the transition, then inserts the TLS MITM path
without replacing the underlying logical session.
.PP
Detection depends on seeing a supported and correctly ordered protocol exchange.
Unknown extensions, encrypted negotiation or traffic which does not match a
signature remains plaintext or opaque according to policy.
.SH SSH MITM
SSH can always be carried as opaque TCP. Builds with libssh support can perform
SSHv2 MITM when a matching policy assigns an
.B ssh_profile .
Smithproxy presents the profile's host key downstream and opens a separate SSH
session upstream.
.PP
The initial SSH MITM path supports password-authenticated session channels.
Features not handled by the SSH layer remain opaque or unsupported. SSHv1 is
not accepted by the MITM path. Clients will observe the Smithproxy host key, so
known-host verification must be deployed deliberately rather than disabled
without review.
.SH QUIC AND HTTP/3 MITM
QUIC carries TLS and multiplexed application streams over UDP. Smithproxy uses a
separate transparent QUIC listener rather than the generic UDP proxy. Full MITM
operation requires OpenSSL 3.5 or newer with QUIC server support and explicit
.B quic_port
and
.B quic_workers
configuration.
.PP
The listener terminates downstream QUIC, negotiates HTTP/3 and creates an
upstream QUIC connection. Individual bidirectional streams are relayed inside a
single connection. HTTP/3 header decoding uses QPACK state associated with that
connection.
.PP
QUIC interception is experimental. The supplied network script can block
configured QUIC ports when the listener is disabled, preventing clients from
silently bypassing a TLS inspection policy by switching from TCP/TLS to HTTP/3.
Inspect runtime state with:
.nf
    diag proxy quic list
.fi
.SH CAPTURE
Capture occurs after decryption where an inspection layer exposes plaintext.
Consequently a capture may contain application data which was encrypted on both
external legs.
.TP
.B SMCap and per-connection PCAP
Write connection data to individual local files.
.TP
.B PCAP single
Append sessions to a rotating local PCAP file.
.TP
.B GRE/SPQ1
Export plaintext packets to a remote collector.
.TP
.B GRE/PCAPNG
Export native PCAPNG records, including format-specific metadata and TLS secret
records where available.
.TP
.B SSLKEYLOG
Write TLS traffic secrets for compatible analyzers. This can permit later
decryption of separately captured wire traffic.
.PP
Opaque pass-through protocols remain encrypted in captures. Capture selection
is controlled by the matching content profile and global capture destination
settings.
.SH ROUTING AND IDENTITY
The upstream connection is a new connection. With
.B nat = auto
it normally uses an address belonging to the Smithproxy host. Transparent source
handling can preserve the original source address, but requires correct policy
routing so return traffic reaches Smithproxy rather than the client directly.
.PP
A routing profile can rewrite the destination, select among multiple targets
and rewrite an exact outbound TLS SNI value. Such changes affect the right leg;
they do not alter what the original client requested on the left leg.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Inspect HTTPS for one client network
.nf
    policy = (
        {
            proto = "tcp";
            src = [ "managed_clients" ];
            dst = [ "any", "any6" ];
            sport = [ "all" ]; dport = [ "https" ];
            action = "accept"; nat = "auto";
            tls_profile = "default";
            detection_profile = "detect";
            content_profile = "metadata_only";
            routing = "none";
        }
    );
.fi
.PP
The managed clients must trust the CA configured by the selected TLS profile.
The content profile independently decides whether payload is written.
.SS Bypass a pinned destination
.nf
    tls_profiles = {
        inspect = { inspect = TRUE; };
        bypass = { inspect = FALSE; };
    };

    policy = (
        { proto = "tcp"; dst = [ "pinned_service" ];
          dport = [ "https" ]; tls_profile = "bypass"; ... },
        { proto = "tcp"; dst = [ "any", "any6" ];
          dport = [ "https" ]; tls_profile = "inspect"; ... }
    );
.fi
.PP
The narrow bypass must precede the broad inspection rule. Fill all required
policy fields represented by the ellipses; they are omitted only for clarity.
.SS Compare opaque and inspected captures
Capture the same test request on the client-facing interface and through a
content profile attached to the inspected session. The wire capture contains
TLS records; the Smithproxy capture can contain HTTP data. These are different
views of two independent TLS legs, not duplicate packet streams.
.SH FAILURE BEHAVIOR
The two-leg model creates failure points which do not exist in a simple packet
forwarder: upstream connection, certificate generation, cryptographic handshake,
certificate validation, protocol parsing and capture/export can fail
independently.
.PP
Use the event list, connection history, live session diagnostics and component
debug topics to identify the failing stage. Avoid enabling high-volume payload
or extreme debug logging permanently on production traffic.
.SH TESTING POLICY
Since October 2026, Smithproxy records successful clean-tree validation as
commit-specific reports under
.IR docs/test-reports/ .
MITM changes are expected to document the exercised protocol and negative paths;
a single successful request is not sufficient evidence for the complete path.
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
MITM inspection changes the end-to-end trust model. Deploy the Smithproxy CA and
SSH host keys only to intended clients. Restrict access to private keys, CLI and
API credentials, captures, SSLKEYLOG output and decrypted debug logs.
.PP
Inspection should be explicitly scoped by policy. A broad catch-all TLS or SSH
MITM rule can expose credentials and private content, break pinned applications
and create regulatory or privacy obligations. Bypass rules should be narrow,
auditable and tested for fail-open or fail-closed behavior.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Protocol support depends on build options and dependency versions. TLS and SSH
MITM cannot transparently defeat application pinning. QUIC/HTTP/3 is
experimental. Unknown application protocols can be transported and captured,
but are not automatically understood merely because their transport is
decrypted.
